    <origin>Owner round 3b (notes/session27-owner-decisions-and-research.md:79-83): "a way to ADD users/contacts/organizations to the No Access list of a record or an organization; a way to SEE who is on it", and "how is No Access enforced and surfaced at the ORGANIZATION level (does sprk_organization carry the components?)". Disposition row 5 and O2 (notes/session27-ux-research-ethical-wall-secure.md:71, :75-76). Absorbs 143's form dependency.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>Make the No Access list manageable where people already manage access. Put NO ACCESS subgrids on the Organization form (walls ON this organization, and this organization DENIED), next to SYSTEM ACCESS and MEMBERS, and on the Contact form (this contact denied). Make the No Access entry form usable: subject (contact, organization or user), object (organization, or a specific record chosen with a picker instead of a typed GUID), and reason. Add real views and a site map entry in the app access administrators use, and set authoring rights per the owner's answer to O2. 143's enforcement keeps running on save, and the per-record "not enforced" outcome (N5) stays visible.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer — Dataverse forms, views, site maps and security roles via solution customization; a field-bound PCF on Fluent v9 shared components; classic form libraries. Fail closed (ADR-003): a saved wall that walls nothing is the failure to design against.</role>

  <goal>(1) The Organization main form has a NO ACCESS section beside SYSTEM ACCESS / MEMBERS with two subgrids: "Ethical walls on this organization" (`sprk_objectorganization` = this) and "This organization denied" (`sprk_subjectorganization` = this). The Contact main form has a NO ACCESS section with "This contact denied" (`sprk_subjectcontact` = this). Each "+ New" opens the entry main form with the lookup prefilled. (2) The sprk_noaccessentry main form shows Subject (contact / organization / user, exactly one), Object (organization, or a record picked through a new field-bound PCF that writes `sprk_objectrecordtype` + `sprk_objectrecordid` + the display column `sprk_objectrecordname`, exactly one), Reason, Status and 143's enforcement outcome. It refuses to save a malformed entry and keeps 143's post-save enforcement. (3) Real views and a site map entry. (4) Authoring and reading rights per O2. (5) The schema doc answers the owner's organization-level question. (6) A manual live gate proves it.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>WHAT THE OWNER ASKED (round 3b, BINDING; session27 note :79-83). A way to ADD users, contacts or organizations to the No Access list of a record or an organization; a way to SEE who is on it; and how No Access works at the ORGANIZATION level. Record-level add and view run through Manage Access (tasks 064/067, gated on Write on the record). This task covers the organization and contact level and the entry itself.

WHAT EXISTS TODAY (research note :18-36, checked live against spaarkedev1 2026-10-01; repo facts re-verified on this branch).
- The table: `sprk_noaccessentry` is ORGANIZATION-owned (`src/solutions/SpaarkeCore/entities/sprk_noaccessentry/entity-schema.md:31`).
  - Subject: exactly one of `sprk_subjectcontact` / `sprk_subjectorganization` (`:56-61`), plus `sprk_subjectsystemuser` after task 143.
  - Object: exactly one of `sprk_objectorganization` or the pair `sprk_objectrecordtype` (lookup → `sprk_recordtype_ref`) + `sprk_objectrecordid` (String(50), GUID as text) (`:63-69`). Matching is by id alone (`:69`).
  - `sprk_reason` is a Memo (`:75`); `sprk_name` is required and entered manually (`:48`).
  - Exactly-one is NOT enforced at schema level; the schema doc says the reader logs and ignores a malformed row, so it denies nothing (`:105-112`). Deactivation lifts the veto (`:113-115`).
  - ⚠️ CODE vs DOC TODAY: the reader classifies only the OBJECT shape (`NoAccessListReader.cs:390-406`: exactly one of object organization / object record type + id, else excluded; an unparseable id is excluded at `:429-436`). It does NOT check the subject shape: the subject is matched by the `$filter` (`:191-206`), so a row naming both a contact and an organization denies BOTH today, and a row with no subject matches nobody. Task 143 criterion 1 makes zero or two-plus subjects malformed (denies nothing, logged). This task's "Incomplete Entries — deny nothing" view is accurate only after that lands (escalation (h)).
  - The subgrid relationships exist (`:134-137`): `sprk_contact_sprk_noaccessentry_subjectcontact`, `sprk_sprk_organization_sprk_noaccessentry_subjectorganization`, `sprk_sprk_organization_sprk_noaccessentry_objectorganization`.
- The reader: `NoAccessListReader.RowSelect` (`Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/NoAccessListReader.cs:180-182`); the record-object filter `sprk_objectrecordid eq '{id}'` uses .NET's lowercase, unbraced "D" format (`:221-222`).
  - Deny candidates are built only for the root entity type being composed (`AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:596-672`, candidate at `:647`); only sprk_project, sprk_matter and sprk_workassignment are composed for access (task 142 background, `CallerPrincipalResolver.cs:520-524`).
  - An object record of any other type is stored and never evaluated. The schema doc's own example 2 (a `sprk_communication` per-child revocation, `:97`) is one such entry.
  - The ethical wall over an object organization covers every root that references it through `sprk_assignedlawfirm1/2` (`ExternalParticipationService.cs:661-667`, `:710`).
- Authoring today: only System Administrator, System Customizer and Service Writer hold Create/Write. "Spaarke Core User" holds GLOBAL Read, including Reason (research :22-28), which conflicts with task 143's rule that a refusal must not reveal the reason. The table is in no site map; no form has a No Access subgrid; the main form ("Information") holds only Name; the views are the generated defaults; 0 entries in dev.
- O2 (owner, 2026-10-01, research note :74): ACCEPTED as recommended. Remove Read on `sprk_noaccessentry` from Spaarke Core User; a dedicated access-administrator role holds Read/Write; everyone else sees task 153's banner; Write-holders see a record's entries through Manage Access (064's entries tier, the BFF reading on their behalf).
- 064's UX amendment (last paragraph) warns that standard subgrids read the table directly and show nothing to non-administrators once O2 removes core users' Read, and asks whether 154 must read through 064's routes. Under the accepted O2 it need not: the NO ACCESS sections are the access administrators' authoring surface and they hold table Read; non-administrators get 153's banner, and constraint "no false empty" keeps them from seeing an empty grid. No BFF read is added here.
- The Organization form has SYSTEM ACCESS (Access Permission Grant, Standing Grant), MEMBERS (contact memberships) and a hidden To Dos tab, and no No Access components (research :29-31). Forms and site maps are NOT in source control: a Grep of src/ finds no sprk_organization form XML and no site map; the only site-map precedent is `infrastructure/dataverse/solutions/EventsSitemap/Deploy-EventsSitemap.ps1`. The documentation precedent for views/subgrids/quick-create is `src/solutions/SpaarkeCore/entities/sprk_externalrecordaccess/views-schema.md` (`:170-231`).
- Task 143 (BINDING contract this task keeps):
  - it adds `sprk_subjectsystemuser`, `POST /api/v1/external-access/no-access/enforce {entryId}`, and `src/solutions/webresources/sprk_noaccessentry_postsave.js` (sprk_noaccessentry main form OnPostSave → enforce);
  - the enforce route is gated by `DelegationRuleFilter` on caller Write on the ENTRY row (143 constraint "enforce endpoint");
  - per N5 (owner round 3b), removal is enforced only on records where the entry's author holds Write; elsewhere the entry is saved as "not enforced" with a notice (143 amendment R3b). For an organization wall this is decided per covered record;
  - SEQUENCING (143 amendment n="UX"): 143's server work does not wait for this task; its form-registered post-save script, its ui-test and its live gate 14 (ii)-(v) DO wait for this task's form. "Whichever of the two lands second puts [`sprk_subjectsystemuser`] on 154's form"; since this task follows 143's schema step, this task puts it there.
- WHY A PICKER IS NOT A REUSE OF RegardingResolver. `src/client/pcf/RegardingResolver` is bound to `sprk_regardingrecordtype` (`RegardingResolver/ControlManifest.Input.xml:7`). It writes fixed `sprk_regarding*` columns, an entity-specific lookup and the FR-26 core-ancestor stamp (`:3`; `handlers/ResolverWriteHandler.ts:350` `applyRegardingSelection`, `:456-459`, `:485-525`). On a No Access entry those columns do not exist and a core-ancestor stamp would be wrong. The reusable pieces are the shared ones:
  - `DataverseLookupField` (`src/client/shared/Spaarke.UI.Components/src/components/LookupField/DataverseLookupField.tsx:1-60`; opens `Xrm.Utility.lookupObjects` via `INavigationService.openLookup`, Fluent v9, tokens only);
  - `resolveRecordType` (`services/PolymorphicResolverService.ts:161-187`, `sprk_recordtype_ref` by logical name, returns null on failure);
  - `cleanGuid` (`:367-370`, lowercase, unbraced).
- WRITE-PATH RULES. `docs/architecture/DATAVERSE-WRITE-PATH-ARCHITECTURE.md` §5: every write-path invariant has one server owner (WP-1); client checks are preview only (WP-2). The No Access entry shape ("exactly one subject, exactly one object") has no registry row. Its server-side handling today is the reader's malformed-row rule plus (after 064) the endpoint's validation.</background>
