  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="ADR-003 — no silent walls">Scope: the entry form, the picker and every MDA path that creates an entry.
- An entry saved from MDA has exactly one subject (contact, organization or user) and exactly one object (an organization, or a COMPLETE record type + id pair). A half pair (type without id, or id without type) and an id that is not a GUID are malformed too (`NoAccessListReader.cs:390-406`, `:429-436`). The form's OnSave handler prevents a save that breaks this (`getEventArgs().preventDefault()`), with a form notification naming the problem.
- The OnSave check is a PREVIEW (WP-2). The reader's malformed-row rule and 064's validation stay the server-side owners, and the new §5 registry row says so.
- Every MDA form that can create an entry carries 143's post-save enforce call. Quick create is turned OFF for sprk_noaccessentry, so a subgrid "+ New" opens the main form; verify live.</constraint>
    <constraint source="picker contract">Scope: the ObjectRecordPicker PCF.
- It offers ONLY the object types some deny reader evaluates, verified in step 1. Today that is sprk_project, sprk_matter and sprk_workassignment (`AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:596-672`); see escalation (d) for child types.
- It writes, through bound outputs only (no `Xrm.WebApi` side writes, no core-ancestor stamp, no entity-specific lookup):
  - `sprk_objectrecordtype` = the `sprk_recordtype_ref` row for the chosen type (`resolveRecordType`);
  - `sprk_objectrecordid` = the picked id through `cleanGuid` (lowercase, unbraced, matching `NoAccessListReader.cs:221-222`);
  - `sprk_objectrecordname` = the record's display name.
- If the record type cannot be resolved, it writes NOTHING and shows an inline error. It never writes a half pair.
- Choosing a record object clears `sprk_objectorganization` (through its bound output), and the form library's OnChange on `sprk_objectorganization` clears the record pair and name when an organization is chosen, so exactly one object survives. The OnSave check still runs either way.
- Read-only rendering when the form or field is disabled.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §11 — display column only">Scope: `sprk_objectrecordname`.
- A display snapshot for views, subgrids and the form, created under the Spaarke publisher in SpaarkeCore and verified with describe().
- It is NEVER added to `NoAccessListReader.RowSelect` (`:180-182`) and never used for matching. The reader and its fail-closed behaviour are unchanged.
- The schema doc states that it is a snapshot (it can be stale after a rename) and not a key.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner round 3b — placement">Scope: the Organization and Contact forms.
- The Organization NO ACCESS section sits on the same tab as SYSTEM ACCESS and MEMBERS, after MEMBERS, with exactly two subgrids:
  - "Ethical walls on this organization" (relationship `…_objectorganization`; columns: Name, Subject Contact, Subject Organization, Subject User, Status, Created By, Created On);
  - "This organization denied" (relationship `…_subjectorganization`; columns: Name, Object Organization, Object Record Type, Object Record, Status, Created By, Created On).
- The Contact NO ACCESS section has one subgrid, "This contact denied" (relationship `…_subjectcontact`; columns as "This organization denied"). It sits on the tab holding the contact's organization memberships / access components, as found in step 1; if no such tab exists, it goes after the first tab's sections and the notes record that.
- Subgrids show active entries by default, and "+ New" prefills the relationship's lookup.
- Reason is not a subgrid column. It is on the entry form only, governed by O2.</constraint>
    <constraint source="views (closed set)">Scope: sprk_noaccessentry.
- Public views:
  - "Active No Access Entries" (default; columns: Name, Subject Contact, Subject Organization, Subject User, Object Organization, Object Record Type, Object Record, Created By, Created On);
  - "Inactive No Access Entries" (same columns plus Modified On);
  - "Ethical Walls (Organization Objects)" (active, object organization not null);
  - "Record-level Entries" (active, object record id not null);
  - "Incomplete Entries — deny nothing" (active rows with zero subjects, more than one subject, zero objects, both object forms, or a half record pair). This is the reader's malformed-row set once 143's three-subject rule has landed (escalation (h)), visible to administrators. An object record id that is present but not a GUID is also malformed but cannot be expressed in a view filter; the form's OnSave check is its only preview.
- The subgrid views of the placement constraint.
- The lookup view (Name, Subject …, Object …).
- If 143 persists the N5 enforcement outcome on the row, it appears as a column in "Active No Access Entries" and on the form (read-only).
- Every view is documented in views-forms-schema.md.</constraint>
    <constraint source="site map">Scope: the app chosen in step 1. Add a "No Access Entries" subarea (default view "Active No Access Entries") to the site map of the model-driven app(s) whose site map lists Access Permission Grants (`sprk_externalrecordaccess`) or Organizations. If none or more than one app qualifies, escalation (c) applies. Under the accepted O2, the subarea is effectively for the access-administrator role: MDA hides a subarea whose table the user cannot read. Verify that live; if a non-administrator still sees the subarea, set its privilege requirement explicitly rather than leaving it.</constraint>
    <constraint source="O2 (owner answer 2026-10-01: ACCEPTED as recommended) — authoring and reading rights">Scope: security roles. Apply ONLY the owner's answer to O2, as recorded in research note :74 and confirmed at step 0; record each affected role's privileges on sprk_noaccessentry before and after in the notes.
- The accepted recommendation:
  - "Spaarke Core User" (and every other non-administrator role that step 1 finds holding Read) loses Read on sprk_noaccessentry;
  - a new "Spaarke Access Administrator" role holds Create, Read, Write, Append and AppendTo on sprk_noaccessentry at Organization depth, plus the AppendTo on contact, sprk_organization, systemuser and sprk_recordtype_ref needed to set its lookups; no Delete (deactivation is how a wall is lifted);
  - the BFF app identity's reads are unaffected (its app users hold System Administrator, owner-decisions note :101) — prove it with a live deny-read after the change;
  - the new role is assigned IN ADDITION to a user's existing roles, which already supply Read on the lookup target tables.
- Never remove System Administrator from anyone. Never relocate users between BUs.</constraint>
    <constraint source="no false empty (fail closed)">Scope: the Organization and Contact NO ACCESS sections. A user who cannot read entries must never see an empty grid that reads as "nobody is denied". In step 1, observe live how a subgrid renders for a user without Read on the table.
- If the platform hides it or shows a permission message: build nothing.
- If it renders an empty list: add `sprk_noaccess_sections.js`, which on OnLoad shows the section only after a `Xrm.WebApi.retrieveMultipleRecords('sprk_noaccessentry', '?$top=1&amp;$select=sprk_noaccessentryid')` probe succeeds, and hides it on any failure.
- 153's banner remains the signal for everyone.</constraint>
    <constraint source="N5 (owner round 3b) — enforcement per record">Scope: the entry form. This task does not change enforcement. It keeps 143's post-save enforce call and its "not enforced" notice working on the redesigned form. For an organization wall, the notice and the persisted outcome report per covered record (enforced, or not enforced because the author lacks Write). Verify both in the live gate. Do NOT create a second store for the outcome; see escalation (e).</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-006 / ADR-021 / ADR-022 / webresource.md">Scope: UI.
- The picker is a PCF (form binding is required), React 16 platform libraries, Fluent v9 semantic tokens only, dark-mode correct, and mirrors `DataverseLookupField`'s chip-and-select look.
- Form scripts are event handlers only: `"use strict"`, a `Spaarke.*` namespace, try/catch, the form never breaks.
- The form's default-name suggestion fills `sprk_name` only when it is empty ("{subject} — {object}") and stays editable.</constraint>
    <constraint source="owner D-1 / ADR-002">Scope: whole task. No Dataverse plugin, flow, business-rule-as-enforcement or service endpoint. No Dataverse test in CI: live proof is the MANUAL gate with existing non-admin test users in their current BUs. Deploy the schema (new column) BEFORE the PCF that writes it.</constraint>
    <constraint source="CLAUDE.md §10">Scope: this task's PR. This task changes no BFF code. If any step turns out to need a BFF change, STOP and escalate rather than adding it here.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-038">Scope: tests. Component tests for the picker cover the write contract (normalized id, the pair is written together or not at all, the object forms are mutually exclusive, the type list is the verified set, read-only rendering). No `Mock&lt;HttpMessageHandler&gt;`, no DI-registration or ctor null-check tests.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <justification>
    <existing>(1) Picker: the only polymorphic record picker PCF is RegardingResolver, which binds `sprk_regardingrecordtype` and writes `sprk_regarding*`, an entity-specific lookup and the core-ancestor stamp (`ControlManifest.Input.xml:3,:7`; `ResolverWriteHandler.ts:350`, `:456-459`). The shared `DataverseLookupField` (`LookupField/DataverseLookupField.tsx`) is a component, not a form-bound control. (2) Column: sprk_noaccessentry has no display column for the object record (entity-schema.md:63-69); views can show only the GUID text. (3) Form library: 143's `sprk_noaccessentry_postsave.js` is the sprk_noaccessentry form library; it is extended, not duplicated. (4) Section script: nothing on the Organization or Contact forms decides section visibility today (no authored form script on either is in src/). (5) Role: no Spaarke role is scoped to ethical-wall administration; only System Administrator, System Customizer and Service Writer can create entries (research :22).</existing>
    <extension>(1) Extending RegardingResolver would put a second, opposite write contract into its write handler: no stamp, no lookup, different columns — a divergent responsibility (CLAUDE.md §11.5). The new PCF composes the shared `DataverseLookupField`, `resolveRecordType` and `cleanGuid` instead. (2) The display column cannot be derived at view time: `sprk_objectrecordid` is text, not a lookup, so views cannot join to the record. (3) The form library is extended. (4) The section script is conditional on a live observation (constraint "no false empty"). (5) The role exists only under O2's recommendation.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Today nobody but administrators can author an entry. Even they must type a GUID into `sprk_objectrecordid` on a form that shows only Name, and an id with braces or an unpickable type saves a wall that the reader never matches: a silent fail-open ethical wall. Organization walls and denied organizations are invisible on the Organization form. Every core user can read every entry's Reason (research :28), contradicting task 143's refusal rule. The owner's round-3b "add / see" requirement fails for organizations and contacts.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

