  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Declare rigor FULL. Confirm 143's server and schema steps and 064 are merged; read 143's form library (or record that it does not exist yet, escalation (g)), its enforce contract, its malformed-row rule (escalation (h)) and where it persists the N5 outcome. Confirm that the O2 answer recorded in research note :74 (ACCEPTED as recommended) still stands, and record it in notes/task-154-no-access-management-forms.md.</step>
    <step order="1">Live, read-only inventory (spaarkedev1), recorded in the notes:
- the main forms of sprk_organization and contact used by the Spaarke app(s), and where SYSTEM ACCESS / MEMBERS sit;
- the app modules whose site maps list sprk_externalrecordaccess or sprk_organization;
- every security role's privileges on sprk_noaccessentry;
- whether quick create is enabled for the table;
- how a subgrid on a table the user cannot read renders, observed for an existing non-admin test user;
- the object types any deny reader evaluates (Grep of src/server for `NoAccessCandidateRecord` and `GetDeniedRecordsAsync` callers).
Also Grep src/client for any client reader of sprk_noaccessentry: O2 must not break one.</step>
    <step order="2" name="schema (prescriptive within the step)">Create `sprk_objectrecordname` (String, display-only) under the Spaarke publisher in SpaarkeCore, publish it, verify with describe(), and update entity-schema.md. Turn off quick create for sprk_noaccessentry and verify.</step>
    <step order="3">Build the ObjectRecordPicker PCF (shared components, bound outputs, verified type list) with component tests; `npm run build:prod`; deploy via the pcf-deploy skill.</step>
    <step order="4">Rebuild the sprk_noaccessentry main form: Subject section (contact / organization / user — this task places `sprk_subjectsystemuser`, per 143's sequencing), Object section (organization, or the picker bound to the record pair + name), Reason, Status, and 143's outcome if persisted. Keep 143's library registration (after `sprk_/scripts/bff_auth.js`), or create the shared library per escalation (g), and add the OnSave shape check, the OnChange mutual exclusion and the name suggestion. Build the views. Publish.</step>
    <step order="5">Organization and Contact forms: add the NO ACCESS sections and subgrids per the placement constraint. Add the site map subarea to the app chosen in step 1. Add `sprk_noaccess_sections.js` only if step 1 observed an empty-list render. Export to and import from SpaarkeCore via the dataverse-deploy skill. Write views-forms-schema.md.</step>
    <step order="6">Apply O2 as confirmed in step 0: the role changes, record before/after privileges, and prove the BFF deny read still works live (an existing walled contact is still denied on the SPA plane).</step>
    <step order="7">Docs: add to entity-schema.md a section "How No Access works at the organization level", covering:
- subject organization → every ACTIVE member contact (and, per 143 (b), a linked internal user) is denied;
- object organization → an ethical wall over every project, matter and work assignment referencing it in any organization lookup (today `sprk_assignedlawfirm1/2`);
- enforcement on secure records per covered record, per N5;
- the read-time veto on the Teams/SPA planes.
Also add the §5 invariant-registry row to DATAVERSE-WRITE-PATH-ARCHITECTURE.md.</step>
    <step order="8">MANUAL live gate (criterion 11). Record the evidence in the notes file.</step>
    <step order="9">Update TASK-INDEX.md: this task's cell to `✅ [done]`. Record deviations in the notes file.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>(O2) OWNER QUESTION — ANSWERED 2026-10-01: ACCEPTED as recommended (research note :74). The question: should the No Access list, including Reason, stay readable by every core user? Today "Spaarke Core User" holds Global Read on sprk_noaccessentry (research :27-28), so every core user can read every entry's subject, object and Reason, which contradicts task 143's rule that a refusal must not reveal the reason. RECOMMENDATION (accepted): no. (1) Remove Read on sprk_noaccessentry from Spaarke Core User and every other non-administrator role. (2) Grant Create/Read/Write/Append/AppendTo (no Delete) to a dedicated "Spaarke Access Administrator" role. (3) Everyone else sees task 153's banner. (4) Write-holders on a record see and manage that record's entries through Manage Access, where the BFF reads on their behalf (064/067). The trigger still fires, and step 6 STOPS, if: step 0 finds the note no longer records ACCEPTED or the owner has revised O2; step 1 finds a client or server reader that would break when core users lose Read (other than the BFF app identity); or step 1 finds a non-administrator role other than Spaarke Core User holding Read or Write. In each case report what was found, with this recommendation, and wait for the owner. If the owner reverses to keeping Global Read, record that the Reason stays readable to all core users, and skip the role creation and the section script.</trigger>
    <trigger>(b) OPEN OWNER QUESTION — who may author an ORGANIZATION wall from the Organization form? Settled by N5 in principle (research :76): an entry is enforced only on covered records where its author holds Write, per record. Under O2's recommendation, though, only the access-administrator role can create entries at all, so an administrator without Write on a covered record writes a wall that the read-time veto honours but that removes no shares there. If the live gate shows an organization wall that is "not enforced" on most covered records for a typical administrator, STOP and report the counts. Recommendation: accept it (N5 as decided) and make the per-record outcome visible, which this task does.</trigger>
    <trigger>(c) If step 1 finds no app whose site map lists Access Permission Grants or Organizations, or finds more than one, STOP and present the candidates. Recommendation: the app where Access Permission Grants are administered.</trigger>
    <trigger>(d) If step 1 confirms that no deny reader evaluates child object types (today only the three roots, `AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:596-672`), the picker offers the three roots only. Report to the orchestrator that the schema doc's per-child example (`entity-schema.md:97`, a `sprk_communication` revocation) is stored but enforced nowhere. Whether child-level evaluation should be built is an owner decision for a server task. Do not offer a type no reader evaluates.</trigger>
    <trigger>(e) If 143 persists the N5 enforcement outcome nowhere readable (no column, no related row), STOP before step 4's outcome display. The owner's N5 wording is "save as 'not enforced'", which needs a stored state. Report it to the orchestrator rather than inventing a second store here. Likewise, if 143's post-save notice for an organization wall does not report the outcome PER COVERED RECORD, STOP before criterion 10: this task may not change BFF code to add it (constraint "CLAUDE.md §10").</trigger>
    <trigger>(f) If the Organization or Contact main form used by the Spaarke apps differs per app (several main forms), STOP and list them before editing. Recommendation: edit every main form the Spaarke apps expose.</trigger>
    <trigger>(g) If `src/solutions/webresources/sprk_noaccessentry_postsave.js` does not exist when step 4 starts (143 may hold it for its form steps), do NOT create a second library for the same form. Create that file, under 143's file name, web resource name and namespace, with this task's OnSave, OnChange and name-suggestion handlers only. Record the hand-off in the notes and tell the orchestrator, so 143's form steps add the OnPostSave enforce handler to the same file. This mirrors 143's own "whichever lands second" rule. If 143's POML or notes name a different file or namespace by then, follow them.</trigger>
    <trigger>(h) If step 0 finds that 143's malformed-row rule over subjects (143 criterion 1: zero or two-plus subjects deny nothing) has NOT landed, then today's reader still denies BOTH subjects of a two-subject row (`NoAccessListReader.cs:191-206`, `:390-406`), and "Incomplete Entries — deny nothing" would mislabel such rows. STOP before building that view and report it. Recommendation: wait for 143's rule; meanwhile the form's OnSave check prevents new two-subject rows from MDA. Also report that the schema doc's Business Rule 1 (`entity-schema.md:105-110`) claims the subject check already exists.</trigger>
  </escalation>

